      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let $x=3$. Then $\\ln 4=\\ln _{x} 3$ so that $x=\\boxed{3}$.\nCONTRADICTION: No matter what $x$ you choose, $\\log x 4$ is never going to equal $\\log_{27} 3$.\n\nThis forum is expecting a derivative to be taken instead. However for the final answer... $\\boxed{3}$.\n\nSince 4= 2^2 and 27 = 3^3, the answer is 2. **Incorrect**: \n\nBut it is incorrect to claim that base conversion doesn't work here. Check our problem a bit more closely by simply: \n\n$$\\log 27 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3 = 1$$\n$$\\log_{x} 4 = \\log_{x} 2^2 = \\log_{x}2^2 \\Rightarrow 2 \\log_x 2 = 1$$\n$$3 \\Rightarrow \\log_x 2 = \\frac{1}{6}$$\n$$2^x = 3\\Rightarrow x = \\boxed{6}$$\n\nwhy do we have 6 answers on this page, 2 correct but 4 wrong. Did someone get multiple problems wrong? I don't think its fair to downvoted someone for not seeing the solution.\n\nThis is a calculator problem. My calculator showed that $x=6$, I didn't realize that (as I didn't understand what I was asking).\n\nMore seriously, there are at least 4 problem solvers and at lest 5 people reading this so why was this problem an \"easy\" solution problem if 4 people don't got it?\n\nBecause 2 of those \"unable to solve\" came off as trolls ( I'm pretty sure you all have experienced it )? I remembered this problem from last year.\n\nthe derivative is not required (although the identity also must be remembered) expanding Euler's formula you can substitute since both are trigonometric functions..\n2 *((1/2) *log3)*log5 = log8\n2 *5 = log8\nx = 4",
