      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by letting \\(\\log_x 4 = a\\). This implies that \\(x^a = 4\\).\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in a form that relates to \\(a\\). By the change-of-base formula for logarithms, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_b 3}{\\log_b 27}\n\\]\nUsing the fact that \\(27 = 3^3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_b 3}{\\log_b (3^3)} = \\frac{\\log_b 3}{3 \\log_b 3} = \\frac{1}{3 \\log_b 3}\n\\]\nWe can express \\(\\log_b 3\\) using the first equation \\(x^a = 4\\). Specifically, \\(\\log_b 3 = \\frac{\\log_x 3}{\\log_b x}\\). Since \\(x^a = 4\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_b x = \\log_a 4\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\log_b 3 = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{\\log_b x}\n\\]\nSubstituting this back into our expression for \\(\\log_{27} 3\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{3 \\log_b x}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log_a 4 = \\frac{\\log_b 4}{\\log_b a}\\), and \\(\\log_b 4 = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{\\log_b x}\\), we can substitute \\(\\log_b 4\\) as:\n\\[\n\\log_b 4 = \\frac{\\log_b 3 \\cdot \\log_a 4}{\\log_b x} = \\frac{\\frac{\\log_a 4}{3 \\log_b x}}{\\log_b x} = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{3 \\log_b x^2} = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{3 \\cdot 2 \\log_b x} = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{6 \\log_b x}\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n3 \\log_b x\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{3 \\log_b x} = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{3 \\cdot 6 \\log_b x} = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{18 \\log_b x} = \\frac{\\log_a 4}{18 \\cdot \\log_a 4} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis means that \\(a = \\frac{1}{3}\\). Recall that \\(a = \\log_x 4\\), so:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nConverting this back to an exponential form, we get:\n\\[\nx^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 4 \\implies x = 4^3 = 64\n\\]\nThus, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
